<% content_for :title, "Launchpad Case Study" %>
<div id="container" class="case case--launchpad">
    <%= render "home/case_header" %>
    <p class="crumb case-wayfinding">
        <a href="/">Home</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
        <span aria-current="page">Case study</span>
    </p>

    <section id="cs-top">
        <h1>Launchpad</h1>
        <h2>How we made AI ideas tangible early, learned from them quickly, and gave the strongest ones a path to production.</h2>
        <figure class="story-illustration case-header-visual">
            <%= image_tag "launchpad-foundation-shelf.jpg", alt: "Hand-drawn shelf supporting different ideas and forms on a shared foundation" %>
        </figure>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-problem">
        <h3>The cost of uncertainty</h3>
        <p>By late 2025, people across Mod Op were experimenting with generative AI. Ideas showed up in decks, side projects, and demos. We needed a shared way to compare them and decide what deserved more investment.</p>
        <p>A full business case took too long for an early idea, and a polished deck left the important questions open. We needed a cheaper way to learn, one that could work across a roughly 500-person organization and give the strongest ideas a path forward. We had a word for the rough working version cheap enough to build before anyone commits: a pretotype. Launchpad made it the default.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-role">
        <h3>What I owned</h3>
        <p>I started the prototyping program during our Q1 2026 hackathons, then led the product and engineering work that became Launchpad. I owned the evaluation model and the criteria for moving an idea forward. I also designed the platform concept and directed the architecture with engineering.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-insight">
        <h3>The insight</h3>
        <p>I kept seeing the same pattern: once people could try a rough version, the conversation became much more specific. They reacted to the workflow, asked better questions, and showed us whether the idea had any pull.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-system">
        <h3>The process</h3>
        <p>Our sequence was simple: prototype, scope, engineering design, MVP, production. Each stage had to earn the next. The early prototype gave us enough evidence to decide how much time and attention an idea deserved.</p>
        <p>Launchpad gave each prototype a shared home, secure sign-in, a short demo, and a way to collect feedback. Anyone at the agency could suggest an idea, and the product team could see what people were actually using.</p>
        <p>The Q1 hackathons produced dozens of working prototypes across internal tools, client services, subscriptions, and white-label products.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-adoption">
        <h3>How it entered planning</h3>
        <p>Launchpad became part of roadmap planning. Growth teams could see which ideas were getting attention, and the product team could compare that signal with client interest and strategic fit. GEO and Digital Twins were among the first concepts we moved into MVP work.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-commercial">
        <h3>Client consequence</h3>
        <p>The same prototypes changed client conversations. A working version built around the client's brand gave us something concrete to bring into the room and talk through together.</p>
        <p>One session with a beverage client, built on their own brand data and run with product and engineering in the room, led to a scoping engagement and became the model for later client meetings. By August 2026, dozens of client accounts were in active pipeline conversations about concepts that had come through Launchpad.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-learned">
        <figure class="story-illustration">
            <%= image_tag "launchpad-observation-team.jpg", alt: "Hand-drawn team looking across a landscape of ideas and pointing toward a shared direction" %>
        </figure>
        <h3>What I learned</h3>
        <p>I enjoyed making the prototypes. The leadership work came after the demo: deciding what had earned another stage and what had run its course. The program only worked once every idea faced the same criteria, including the ones we liked.</p>
    </section>

